      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF) is a potent proangiogenic cytokine, and it also increases vascular permeability. It is well known that VEGF levels are increased in the airway of asthmatic patients. Hypoxia-inducible factor (HIF) induces a rapid and strong increase in VEGF expression."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "To evaluate the relationship between VEGF level and clinical characteristics and to determine whether VEGF expression is associated with HIF expression in asthmatic patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Bronchoscopy was performed on 30 asthmatic patients and 14 control subjects. The concentration of VEGF in the bronchoalveolar lavage fluid (BALF) was examined using enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay. We measured VEGF, HIF-1alpha, and HIF-2alpha expression on biopsy specimens by means of immunoreactivity."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The VEGF level in the BALF was significantly higher in asthmatic patients than in controls. The VEGF level correlated with eosinophil counts in the BALF in asthmatic subjects (r = 0.501; P < .01). However, the VEGF level did not correlate with percentage of forced expiratory volume in 1 second and airway hyperresponsiveness. Asthmatic patients exhibited higher VEGF, HIF-1alpha, and HIF-2alpha immunoreactivity in the submucosa than did controls. Furthermore, VEGF expression correlated significantly with HIF-1alpha (r = 0.614; P = .02) and HIF-2alpha (r = 0.881; P = .001) expression."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "These findings suggest that VEGF may play an important role in inflammation and that VEGF level is related to HIF in bronchial asthma."
        }
      ]},
